On Radix, mio waits with poll(2), and its poll back end drops an
interest once it has reported it, until the source is registered again
(mio's IoSource types do that when an operation would block). crossterm
reads the terminal and signal-hook drains its socket straight from the
file descriptors, so each source poll reports is registered again, and
before any of them is handled: the handling returns with the first
event, so a source reported beside it was polled no more. A resize that
came with keys, or with the answer to a cursor query, left the terminal
unread for good, and the shell took no more keys.

diff --git a/crossterm-0.29.0/src/event/source/unix/mio.rs b/crossterm-0.29.0/src/event/source/unix/mio.rs
